\newcommand{\No}{\mathbf{No}}
\newcommand{\Zh}{\mathbb{Z}\!\left[\tfrac12\right]}
\newcommand{\birth}{\operatorname{birth}}

\title{Dyadic Surreal Numbers and Finite Birthdays:\\
The Arithmetic Backbone of Conway's Hierarchy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Conway's surreal numbers $\No$ form a proper-class, real-closed ordered
field that simultaneously contains the real numbers, the ordinal numbers,
and a rich hierarchy of infinite and infinitesimal quantities. Each surreal
number carries a \emph{birthday}, an ordinal recording the stage at which it
is created in Conway's inductive construction. A guiding theme is that the
surreals \emph{born on finite days} are exactly the dyadic rationals
$\Zh = \{ m/2^n : m \in \mathbb{Z},\ n \in \mathbb{N}\}$. This paper
develops the arithmetic core of that picture. We compute the exact birthday
of every power of one half, $\birth(2^{-n}) = n+1$, and deduce that all such
powers are born strictly before the first infinite day $\omega$. We
establish that the powers of one half form a strictly decreasing sequence of
distinct positive elements satisfying the rescaling law $2^n \cdot 2^{-n} =
1$ and the exponent law $2^{-m}\cdot 2^{-n} = 2^{-(m+n)}$. Building on
these, we prove that the canonical map $\Zh \to \No$ is an injective ring
homomorphism, so that the dyadic rationals embed faithfully into the
surreals both additively and multiplicatively; consequently $\Zh$ is
ring-isomorphic to the subring of dyadic surreals. We close by explaining
how this finite-birthday layer anchors the broader program of describing
$\No$ as a field of Hahn series, and record that not every rational---%
$\tfrac13$ in particular---has a finite birthday.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Numbers with birthdays}
The surreal numbers arose from John Conway's analysis of two-player
combinatorial games. Their defining feature is a recursive construction in
which every number is assembled from previously constructed numbers.

\begin{definition}[Surreal number, informal]
A surreal number is an ordered pair $x = \{\, L \mid R \,\}$ of sets $L, R$
of previously constructed surreal numbers such that no element of $L$ is
$\geq$ any element of $R$. Order, equality, addition, and multiplication are
all defined by simultaneous recursion on this construction.
\end{definition}

The recursion is anchored at the empty stage, which produces
$0 = \{\ \mid\ \}$. Each surreal number $x$ is assigned an ordinal
\emph{birthday} $\birth(x)$, the least stage at which a representative of $x$
appears; equivalently, one more than the supremum of the birthdays of the
options of the simplest representative of $x$.

\begin{definition}[Finite-birthday surreals]
Write $\No_\omega$ for the collection of surreal numbers $x$ with
$\birth(x) < \omega$. These are the numbers created on the finite days
$0, 1, 2, \ldots$
\end{definition}

The organizing conjecture of the subject, essentially due to Conway, is that
$\No_\omega = \Zh$, the ring of dyadic rationals.

\subsection{What this paper proves}
We do not settle the full identity $\No_\omega = \Zh$ (the converse
containment requires the simplicity theorem; see \S\ref{sec:disc}). Instead
we establish the arithmetic backbone that makes it meaningful: that $\Zh$
lives faithfully inside $\No$ with the expected finite birthdays. The main
contributions are: exact birthdays $\birth(2^{-n}) = n+1$; the order and
scaling properties of the half-powers; injectivity of the canonical dyadic
map with the induced additive isomorphism; and multiplicativity yielding a
faithful ring embedding $\Zh \hookrightarrow \No$.

\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection{The ordered field of surreals}
We take as given that $\No$, modulo the equivalence ``$x = y$ iff $x \le y$
and $y \le x$,'' is a totally ordered field: addition, negation, and
multiplication are well defined, associative, commutative, and distributive;
$0$ and $1$ are the identities; every nonzero element is invertible; and the
order is compatible with the operations. In particular $\No$ is an
\emph{integral domain}: if $ab = 0$ then $a = 0$ or $b = 0$.

\subsection{The powers of one half}
\begin{definition}[Powers of one half]\label{def:ph}
Define $2^{-n}$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$ recursively by
\[
2^{-0} = 1, \qquad 2^{-(n+1)} = \bigl\{\, 0 \ \big|\ 2^{-n} \,\bigr\}.
\]
Thus $2^{-1} = \{0 \mid 1\} = \tfrac12$, $2^{-2} = \{0 \mid \tfrac12\} =
\tfrac14$, and so on.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dyadic rationals]
The ring of dyadic rationals is $\Zh = \{ m/2^n : m \in \mathbb{Z},\ n \in
\mathbb{N} \}$, canonically the localization of $\mathbb{Z}$ at the powers
of $2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical dyadic map]\label{def:phi}
Let $\Phi : \Zh \to \No$ be determined by $\Phi(m/2^n) = m \cdot 2^{-n}$,
where $m \cdot (\ )$ is the $m$-fold additive multiple. By construction
$\Phi$ is an additive homomorphism.
\end{definition}

\section{Birthdays of the powers of one half}

\begin{theorem}[Exact birthday]\label{thm:birth}
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $\ \birth(2^{-n}) = n + 1.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$. For $n = 0$, $2^{-0} = 1 = \{0 \mid\ \}$ is built from the
option $0$ (born on day $0$), so its birthday is $1$. For the step,
$2^{-(n+1)} = \{0 \mid 2^{-n}\}$ has options $0$ (birthday $0$) and $2^{-n}$
(birthday $n+1$ by hypothesis); the birthday of a numeric game is the
successor of the supremum of the birthdays of the options of its simplest
form, namely $(n+1)+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite birthday]\label{cor:fin}
For every $n$, $\birth(2^{-n}) < \omega$; hence every power of one half lies
in $\No_\omega$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Order structure of the half-powers}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity]\label{thm:pos}
For every $n$, $\ 0 < 2^{-n}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct with Definition~\ref{def:ph}. $2^{-(n+1)} = \{0 \mid 2^{-n}\}$ has $0$
as a left option, so it is $\ge 0$; strictness follows since its right
option $2^{-n}$ is positive by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonvanishing]\label{cor:nz}
$2^{-n} \ne 0$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Strict decrease]\label{thm:dec}
For every $n$, $\ 2^{-(n+1)} < 2^{-n}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$2^{-(n+1)} = \{0 \mid 2^{-n}\}$ has $2^{-n}$ as a right option, and a
numeric game is strictly less than each of its right options.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Antitonicity and distinctness]\label{thm:anti}
The map $n \mapsto 2^{-n}$ is strictly decreasing, hence injective; the
values $1 > \tfrac12 > \tfrac14 > \cdots$ are pairwise distinct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rescaling law]\label{thm:scale}
For every $n$, $\ 2^{n} \cdot 2^{-n} = 1.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This reflects $2^{-n}$ being the reciprocal of $2^n$; it follows from the
recursive identity $2 \cdot 2^{-(n+1)} = 2^{-n}$ and induction.
\end{proof}

\section{The dyadic rationals embed additively}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity]\label{thm:inj}
The additive homomorphism $\Phi : \Zh \to \No$ of
Definition~\ref{def:phi} is injective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As $\Phi$ is a group homomorphism, injectivity is equivalent to triviality
of the kernel. Write $q = m/2^n$, so $\Phi(q) = (m:\No)\cdot 2^{-n}$. If this
is $0$, then since $\No$ is an integral domain, either $(m:\No) = 0$ or
$2^{-n} = 0$. The latter is impossible by Corollary~\ref{cor:nz}, so
$(m:\No)=0$, whence $m=0$ and $q = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Dyadic subgroup]
Let $D := \operatorname{im}\Phi \subseteq \No$, an additive subgroup; its
elements are the \emph{dyadic surreals}.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mem}
$x \in D$ iff $x = \Phi(q)$ for some $q \in \Zh$; the set $D$ equals the
range of $\Phi$; and $2^{-n} = \Phi(1/2^n) \in D$ for every $n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Additive isomorphism]\label{thm:addiso}
$\Phi$ induces an isomorphism of additive groups $\Zh \xrightarrow{\cong} D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Phi$ surjects onto its image $D$ and is injective by
Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}; an injective surjective homomorphism is an
isomorphism onto its image.
\end{proof}

\section{Multiplicativity and the ring embedding}

\begin{theorem}[Exponent law]\label{thm:exp}
For all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$, $\ 2^{-m} \cdot 2^{-n} = 2^{-(m+n)}.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Multiply both sides by the nonzero scalar $2^{m+n}$. On the left,
\[
2^{m+n}\bigl(2^{-m} 2^{-n}\bigr) = (2^m 2^{-m})(2^n 2^{-n}) = 1\cdot 1 = 1
\]
by Theorem~\ref{thm:scale}; on the right, $2^{m+n} 2^{-(m+n)} = 1$
likewise. Since $2^{m+n} \ne 0$ and $\No$ is a field, multiplication by
$2^{m+n}$ is injective, so the original sides are equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unitality]\label{thm:unit}
$\Phi(1) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The unit $1 \in \Zh$ is $1/2^0$, and $\Phi(1/2^0) = 1 \cdot 2^{-0} = 1$, a
special case of the exponent law with $m=n=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity]\label{thm:mul}
For all $x, y \in \Zh$, $\ \Phi(x y) = \Phi(x)\Phi(y).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $x = a/2^m$, $y = b/2^n$, we have $xy = ab/2^{m+n}$, so $\Phi(xy) =
(ab)2^{-(m+n)}$. On the other side, by bilinearity of surreal
multiplication over integer scaling and the exponent law,
$\Phi(x)\Phi(y) = (a 2^{-m})(b 2^{-n}) = (ab)(2^{-m} 2^{-n}) =
(ab)2^{-(m+n)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic ring embedding]\label{thm:ring}
$\Phi$ is an injective ring homomorphism $\Zh \hookrightarrow \No$; its image
$D$ is a subring, and $\Phi$ induces a ring isomorphism $\Zh
\xrightarrow{\cong} D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Phi$ is additive (Definition~\ref{def:phi}), unital
(Theorem~\ref{thm:unit}), and multiplicative (Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}), hence
a ring homomorphism; it is injective by Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}. An injective
ring homomorphism is an isomorphism onto its image, and the image of a ring
homomorphism is a subring.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{Not every rational is dyadic}
It is tempting to conjecture that $\No_\omega$ contains all rationals. This
is false: $\tfrac13$ has no finite birthday, arising only at day $\omega$ as
the common limit $\{\tfrac14, \tfrac{5}{16}, \ldots \mid \tfrac12, \tfrac38,
\ldots\}$ of its binary approximations. The surreal construction inserts
simplest midpoints, and midpoints of dyadic endpoints are dyadic; only
binary subdivision occurs at finite stages, so $\No_\omega$ is exactly $\Zh$
and nothing larger.

\subsection{The road to the reals and beyond}
The results here are the base of a tower: extend $\Phi$ to an ordered-field
embedding of $\mathbb{R}$ (realized by day $\omega$ via dyadic cuts),
introduce the smallest positive infinitesimal $\varepsilon = \{0 \mid 1,
\tfrac12, \tfrac14, \ldots\}$ at day $\omega$, and reach the Conway--Gonshor
normal form $\No \cong \mathbb{R}((t^{\No}))$. The finite-birthday dyadics
are the seed crystal from which the whole edifice grows.

\subsection{An ordinal-strength remark}
Injectivity of $\Phi$ and the isomorphism $\Zh \cong D$ show that a
countable field sits faithfully inside the proper-class field $\No$, entirely
within the finite-birthday fragment: all of dyadic arithmetic is visible in
the first $\omega$ days.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove $\No_\omega = \Zh$ via the simplicity theorem and the birthday
bounds proved here; note $\tfrac13 \notin \No_\omega$.
\item Extend $\Phi$ to an ordered-field embedding $\mathbb{R} \hookrightarrow
\No$ along dyadic cuts.
\item Characterize the birthday of a general dyadic surreal $m \cdot 2^{-n}$.
\item Formalize $\varepsilon = \{0 \mid 1, \tfrac12, \tfrac14, \ldots\}$,
prove $0 < \varepsilon < 2^{-n}$ for all $n$, and study $\No_{\omega\cdot 2}$
and $\No_{\omega^2}$ toward the Hahn-series description of $\No$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
We established exact birthdays $\birth(2^{-n}) = n+1$, the order and scaling
properties of the half-powers, injectivity of the canonical dyadic map, and
its multiplicativity, yielding a faithful ring embedding $\Zh \hookrightarrow
\No$ with image an isomorphic subring. The dyadic rationals live inside
Conway's number-universe exactly as expected, setting the stage for
embedding the reals and charting the infinitesimal layers beyond day
$\omega$.

